FreeJ installation instructions
===================================

Maintaining the Linux native build
-----------------------------------

Use Debian 13 amd64 and a scratch path without spaces outside the checkout. Choose
the compatibility profile for directory plugins, including third-party
frei0r and FreeFrame. Choose the static profile for the fully archived
Linux CLI with the built-in frei0r bundle. Set up the
pinned compiler, SDKs and Bash environment from doc/native-profiles.md.
The installed freej.pc records the profile and selected capabilities.

For a decoder edit, use the compatibility environment from that guide.
From the checkout root, run the following in Bash. The build script
exports the checkout to a new directory and prints its build and stage
locations. The touch schedules a decoder rebuild for a rehearsal with
unchanged source. An actual edit to that file in the build's source copy
also schedules the rebuild.

    repo=$PWD
    scratch=$(mktemp -d)
    export FREEJ_BUILD_WORK="$scratch/compatibility"
    tests/freej-build-test.sh --enable-static-mozjs --with-native-profile=compatibility
    build="$FREEJ_BUILD_WORK/build"
    prefix="$FREEJ_BUILD_WORK/install"
    stage="$FREEJ_BUILD_WORK/stage$prefix"
    touch "$FREEJ_BUILD_WORK/source/src/media/movie_decoder.cpp"
    make -C "$build/src" libmovie_decoder.la libfreej.la
    make -C "$build/tests" check TESTS=movie-replay-test
    make -C "$build" install DESTDIR="$FREEJ_BUILD_WORK/stage"

Build the consumer against only the staged FreeJ package and use the SDK
library paths you set for the compatibility build. The pkg-config
override points the development flags at the DESTDIR copy.

    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$stage/lib/pkgconfig"
    pkg-config --variable=profile freej
    pkg-config --variable=capabilities freej
    read -r -a engine_cflags <<< "$(pkg-config --define-variable="prefix=$stage" --cflags freej)"
    read -r -a engine_libs <<< "$(pkg-config --define-variable="prefix=$stage" --libs freej)"
    $CXX -DNDEBUG "${engine_cflags[@]}" -c "$repo/tests/native-profile-factory.cpp" -o "$scratch/factory.o"
    "$legacy/gcc12/usr/bin/g++-12" "$scratch/factory.o" "${engine_libs[@]}" -o "$scratch/factory"
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$stage/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH" SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy "$scratch/factory"

Update one dependency family at a time in the lock files listed in
doc/native-profiles.md and doc/native-dependencies.md. Save the accepted
revision and SDK. For the movie SDK, change the URL and SHA-256 in
tests/movie-linux-sdk.lock in an expendable checkout. Verify the upstream
release and its signature before recording its digest. In the same Bash
environment, run tests/movie-linux-sdk.sh compatibility
"$scratch/movie-candidate". Compare the changed SDK to the accepted one
using the same movie fixture and profile options.

Restore the accepted pin in the expendable checkout. For this example
accepted is the full commit ID recorded before changing the pin. If you
have the accepted tarball locally, point FREEJ_MOVIE_SDK_TARBALL at its
absolute path before running the recipe. The recipe verifies the tarball
against the restored lock before it extracts or builds any source.

    git restore --source="$accepted" -- tests/movie-linux-sdk.lock
    tests/movie-linux-sdk.sh compatibility "$scratch/movie-restored"
    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$scratch/movie-restored/sdk/lib/pkgconfig:$debian_lib/pkgconfig"
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$scratch/movie-restored/sdk/lib:$debian_lib"
    FREEJ_BUILD_WORK="$scratch/rollback-check" \
        tests/freej-build-test.sh --enable-static-mozjs --with-native-profile=compatibility

Use doc/native-ci.md for the full two-profile gate, including the installed
package, software replays and effect-frame comparison.
For a pin change, rerun both profiles in fresh paths before accepting it.
API maps the layer, controller and effect sources to their focused tests.

Historical installation notes
-----------------------------

*** x86 PC (for Darwin/OSX see README.OSX)


[*] be sure to have all the required libraries and tools:
 cdbs, libtool, debhelper (>> 7.0.0), pkg-config, flex, bison, libsdl-dev,
 libpng-dev, libfreetype6-dev, libfontconfig-dev, libogg-dev, libvorbis-dev,
 libjpeg-dev, libslang2-dev, libtheora-dev, libavcodec-dev, libavformat-dev,
 libswscale-dev, libunicap2-dev, libbluetooth-dev, fftw3-dev, libjack-dev,
 liblo0-dev, libasound-dev, libhtml-template-perl, python-dev, swig

- A working GNU/Linux system with X or framebuffer video

- SDL libraries http://www.libsdl.org

- PNG library (compile with _ZLIB_ support) http://libpng.org

== to have some more assembler mmx optimized filters you need NASM:
   NASM netwide assembler compiler http://sf.net/projects/nasm
   then go in the filters/nasm-x86 and type make!
   copy by hand the *.so filters in a ~/.freej/ directory in your home

[*] then you are ready to compile!

== download freej latest stable version from http://ftp.dyne.org/freej
   extract the source archive and compile it:
   $ tar xvfz freej.tar.gz
   $ cd freej
    (if you are reading this file from the sourcecode, start from here)
   $ ./configure
   $ make
   # make install

   this will install effect plugins into /usr/local/lib/freej/
   the freej binary in /usr/local/bin/
  (or any other prefix you configured instead of the default /usr/local)

   $ freej [enter]

   and you should see.



:: INSTALL FROM DEBIAN PACKAGES

You can do 'apt-get install freej' and you will have a precompiled FreeJ 

Anyway to squeeze the best out of your box you want to compile this
software with machine specific optimizations!

The source configure script guesses the best compiler optimization flags
for your CPU.

the following commands are then necessary to set your build environment,
from inside the freej source directory do:

sudo apt-get install autoconf automake
sudo apt-get build-dep freej
dpkg-buildpackage -rfakeroot



:: INSTALL FROM GIT REPOSITORY
   (only for developers, skilled users, full code alchemists)

For the historical Linux amd64 reference with pinned compiler and SDKs, see
doc/legacy-reference.md.

Assuming you have Debian/Ubuntu (for other distro there can be some
differences), with all tools needed and all developers lib installed 
(e.g. build-essential, the libs above etc.:

  You need git:
- apt-get install git-core

  So, get the code:
- git clone git://code.dyne.org/freej.git

After this, you will have a "freej" dir. Enter in it, and do 

- ./autogen.sh
- Run "./configure" with your favourite options.
- make && sudo make install 

That's all!



If you want to experiment with live video, please be sure to have a working
camera and to activate it from your BIOS settings (e.g. for eeepc 701), or
it will not work! :)

If you have problems, you are welcome to ask on the mailinglist for
help, there you will probably find some good suggestions

Please try to not mix your system's problems with FreeJ's bugs.

If you find bugs, you are welcome to report them in the bugtracking
system on http://bugs.dyne.org


-jrml, asbesto


